Um, and it may be that it, um, you know, maybe it's worth giving some thought to whether or not this should be split into two ordinances.

48
00:03:49,019 --> 00:03:53,582
One that's covering your chapter 87 public shade trees and one that is covering

49
00:03:54,414 --> 00:03:55,976
trees that are not covered by chapter 87.

50
00:03:56,717 --> 00:04:08,571
So if that was the case, you could work within the existing or chapter 87 and then theoretically or potentially develop a new ordinance for the trees that aren't covered as public shade trees.

51
00:04:09,612 --> 00:04:09,812
Okay.

52
00:04:10,898 --> 00:04:11,238
Thank you.

53
00:04:12,939 --> 00:04:21,403
So going on, we also have opening comment from Commissioner McGibbon that they're very real impacts to DPW contained in this proposed ordinance.

54
00:04:21,583 --> 00:04:25,985
I do not believe it could be implemented successfully with existing DPW resources and structure.

55
00:04:26,145 --> 00:04:35,690
I recommend that the ordinance be evaluated against current human and monetary resources and include an assessment of projected full-time employees required for a successful implementation of this ordinance.

56
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:46,255
My gut tells me somewhere between two and three full time employees that information could be used to initiate a strategy to arrive at that required support to make the implementation of the successful but is not there yet.

57
00:04:50,397 --> 00:04:51,998
And then moving on.

58
00:04:55,279 --> 00:05:00,602
You noted attorney sign if you could speak I think there's reference to this a few spaces as far as.

59
00:05:01,745 --> 00:05:10,888
I think this was a bit of a push-pull in the one meeting we had too, as far as the applicability or the ability to apply this in ordinance to private property trees.

60
00:05:12,348 --> 00:05:12,608
Sure.

61
00:05:12,648 --> 00:05:15,649
So, and again, we definitely discussed this last time.

62
00:05:17,290 --> 00:05:19,551
My job in looking at this is to flag potential issues.

63
00:05:19,611 --> 00:05:30,374
So I'm not saying this is necessarily a problem, but if you're gonna be regulating private property independent of any other permitting scheme,

64
00:05:31,303 --> 00:05:52,464
um you need to have a public purpose in place for that and I don't know yes or no um if just maintaining a mature tree canopy would suffice if this was challenged in court but generally speaking you know if you're going to be regulating or requiring permitting um or mitigation associated with trees on private property there has to be a nexus between

65
00:05:53,024 --> 00:06:04,167
and a rough proportionality between the mitigation, the adverse impacts, you know, it has to be tied to a legitimate public purpose, which this may be, but, you know, it's certainly my job to flag to you potential issues.

66
00:06:04,247 --> 00:06:05,187
That's what I'm doing there.

67
00:06:05,207 --> 00:06:06,947
And I don't know that we need to repeat it as we go.

68
00:06:06,967 --> 00:06:10,128
You're right, there are references throughout as it comes up.

69
00:06:10,188 --> 00:06:22,171
But again, this may be one of the areas where the city might want to think about, do we want to just have a separate ordinance of dealing with kind of a private tree issues versus the chapter 87 public shade tree issues?

70
00:06:22,728 --> 00:06:24,909
and for clarity, it may make sense to split those out.

71
00:06:24,969 --> 00:06:26,049
So just something to think about.

72
00:06:27,310 --> 00:06:28,130
Thank you, Attorney Stein.

73
00:06:28,190 --> 00:06:28,730
Counselor Knight.

74
00:06:34,993 --> 00:06:36,634
Thank you very much, Madam President.

75
00:06:36,834 --> 00:06:46,198
Counselor, I was just wondering maybe would it make sense for us to look at this in a way where, you know, if the intent is to protect the tree canopy publicly, right?

76
00:06:46,498 --> 00:06:51,880
And the tree canopy of the community, and we have private shade trees that, you know, participate in that function.

77
00:06:53,059 --> 00:06:55,081
to limit this to a scope.

78
00:06:55,761 --> 00:07:20,218
I offered an email earlier that would make a recommendation to limit this to maybe residential parcels that are exempt residential parcels over 5,000 square feet that is seeking to remove one or less trees, something like that, or maybe limiting it to parcels that are only subject to site plan review, which are parcels that are a little bit larger

79
00:07:21,085 --> 00:07:22,726
you know, I think there's six units or more.

80
00:07:26,087 --> 00:07:35,750
Will we still face the same complications that you mentioned earlier or would that be something that might change things because they're larger scale parcels?

81
00:07:37,671 --> 00:07:48,055
I mean, ultimately the idea behind this when I brought it up was that there were a couple of developments that were proposed in the community, one of them in

82
00:07:48,918 --> 00:07:55,521
Winthrop Street and the other one up in the Fulton Heights area where they clear cut probably close to 250 trees.

83
00:07:56,982 --> 00:08:01,384
So the idea was that we want to prevent that from happening again on large scale parcels like that.

84
00:08:01,544 --> 00:08:03,525
That was my intent anyway when I brought it forward.

85
00:08:03,845 --> 00:08:04,926
It's changed a little bit since then.

86
00:08:05,306 --> 00:08:13,850
But ultimately, it was to prevent that type of clear cutting on large scale parcels without having some sort of community mitigation and reimbursement back.

87
00:08:14,450 --> 00:08:17,792
So would we face those same concerns that you

88
00:08:18,447 --> 00:08:22,889
had raised even though the parcels would be residential, but larger scale parcels.

89
00:08:25,530 --> 00:08:27,531
So it's something we could take a look at.

90
00:08:27,971 --> 00:08:37,155
I haven't seen your specific email, but we can certainly look at options for limiting this to certain projects of a certain size that may have more of an impact.

91
00:08:37,175 --> 00:08:43,098
So then that would be more tied to the community interest to mitigate against that impact.

92
00:08:45,039 --> 00:08:47,800
Right, because I think the person that's trying

93
00:08:48,528 --> 00:09:07,317
you know, take a tree down out of their front yard because they wanna put a swing set in is a little bit different from the person that wants to, you know, take down three full grown oak trees because they wanna put a different, a second house on their double lot or someone that wants to, you know, add substantial square footage to their, you know, a boat.

94
00:09:07,737 --> 00:09:14,420
So I think that that's really, you know, when you talk about community impact, I think that's right on the nexus that we need to head on.

95
00:09:15,622 --> 00:09:16,942
No, we can certainly take a look at that.

96
00:09:16,962 --> 00:09:20,663
And we can also take a look at how those changes might intersect with existing zoning.

97
00:09:21,463 --> 00:09:26,204
If we just get maybe a little bit more detail about what you folks are thinking, then we can get back to you on it.

98
00:09:28,425 --> 00:09:37,947
And then I also had a question about whether or not, you know, obviously if it was residential, it's residential, but we've been having some discussions about private ways versus public ways.

99
00:09:39,007 --> 00:09:45,248
And would there be any type of differentiation between a tree in a private way versus a tree in a public way?

100
00:09:46,443 --> 00:09:48,484
I mean, there certainly is for the statute.

101
00:09:48,704 --> 00:09:54,025
There's a statute is covering public shade trees, which are defined in there.

102
00:09:54,125 --> 00:10:00,287
And that again, is something that we can take a look at when we have some direction on how you want to regulate those.

103
00:10:03,908 --> 00:10:04,849
Point of information.

104
00:10:05,389 --> 00:10:12,491
Thank you.

105
00:10:13,557 --> 00:10:16,278
Vice President Bear, sorry, I was just switching to AirPods.

106
00:10:17,138 --> 00:10:18,519
My apologies, Madam President.

107
00:10:18,919 --> 00:10:32,263
I just wanted to note for Councilor Knight as well that somewhere in here, Tim McGivern commented that he would be very concerned about the city taking responsibility on private ways when we aren't, you know, that are not accepted.

108
00:10:33,684 --> 00:10:36,905
So I think that would be something we'd really need to take a look at as well.

109
00:10:42,165 --> 00:10:44,887
Councilor Knight, would you like to put any of those recommendations in the form of a motion?

110
00:10:44,907 --> 00:10:52,954
The clerk muted me.

111
00:10:54,555 --> 00:10:57,978
No, ultimately, I think this is something that's gonna be hashed out in further meetings.

112
00:10:58,038 --> 00:11:00,960
I don't think this is gonna be something that's gonna be hitting the council floor after tonight.

113
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:03,542
It's just some, I guess we call it food for thought.

114
00:11:05,343 --> 00:11:06,004
Thank you, Councilor Knight.

115
00:11:06,384 --> 00:11:06,985
Councilor Collins.

116
00:11:10,287 --> 00:11:11,208
Thank you, President Morrell.

117
00:11:14,177 --> 00:11:39,103
I wanted to kind of going off of this same topic, I was curious if the attorney knew perhaps from other communities ordinances on the same topic or just in general, on the point of, you know, we need to clearly articulate the public purpose for which we're regulating potentially private property.

118
00:11:42,995 --> 00:11:57,678
Is there a way that we can sort of semantically reframe what we already have to articulate more clearly the public purpose apart from just maintaining the tree canopy, for example, specifying clean air, protection from soil runoff and flooding, other ecological effects?

119
00:11:59,519 --> 00:12:10,281
I guess I'm curious if just specifying the public purposes in the ordinance would get at that issue of liability in the event of a legal challenge.

120
00:12:11,260 --> 00:12:15,103
on the regulating private parcels side of things.

121
00:12:16,384 --> 00:12:22,488
I certainly think that it would be helpful to include all of the purposes, you know, in detail.

122
00:12:22,508 --> 00:12:39,419
So, you know, in the ordinance, what would happen on the challenge, I can't say, but certainly if there's more reason, then yeah, I would definitely include all of the reasons for, you know, all the public purposes that go with the ordinance.

123
00:12:40,140 --> 00:12:40,780
Yeah, definitely.

124
00:12:43,726 --> 00:12:44,608
Thank you, Councillor Collins.

125
00:12:44,888 --> 00:12:47,633
Amanda Bowen, I see you have your hand up.

126
00:12:47,813 --> 00:12:49,256
Just name and address for the record, please.

127
00:12:53,792 --> 00:12:55,374
Hi, I'm Amanda Bowen.

128
00:12:55,534 --> 00:13:09,648
I'm from Trees Medford and I just wanted to make a quick point, which is that there are numerous municipalities throughout the Commonwealth that have ordinances in place and we borrowed a lot of what we have in here from those.

129
00:13:09,848 --> 00:13:14,233
And so I think it really has to be acknowledged and any work on this document should really

130
00:13:15,073 --> 00:13:19,475
make reference and we'd be happy to share some examples with anybody who's interested.

131
00:13:19,836 --> 00:13:27,719
There are many, many of our surrounding towns that have similar works in documents in place.

132
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:28,880
That's all.

133
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:29,280
Thank you.

134
00:13:30,361 --> 00:13:31,021
Thank you, Amanda.

135
00:13:31,821 --> 00:13:38,925
Attorney Stein, to that point, are you do you have experience or you're seeing ordinances like this be challenged around the state?

136
00:13:39,912 --> 00:13:46,515
You know, it's a little bit different when you're talking cities versus towns, because, so, the dog is barking.

137
00:13:46,535 --> 00:13:51,258
The Attorney General doesn't review ordinances passed by cities.

138
00:13:51,278 --> 00:13:54,199
The Attorney General only reviews bylaws passed by towns.

139
00:13:54,319 --> 00:14:09,167
So I have taken a look through probably, I think when I searched the records back in 2016 on the AG's website, I saw one town that had a similar approach, different in the details.

140
00:14:09,965 --> 00:14:12,146
And I do see the Attorney General's comments on that.

141
00:14:12,707 --> 00:14:14,668
I'm not aware of any court challenges on them.

142
00:14:14,708 --> 00:14:19,190
So there's definitely a lot out there in different versions.

143
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:27,735
And they are very fact specific as far as connecting the level of regulation to the actual community need and the public purpose.

144
00:14:27,815 --> 00:14:34,139
And they don't all regulate to the same degree and extent, but certainly there's examples out there.

145
00:14:34,159 --> 00:14:36,140
There isn't a lot on interpreting them.

146
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:37,781
Thank you, Attorney Stein.

147
00:14:40,383 --> 00:14:44,145
Thank you.

148
00:14:44,185 --> 00:14:45,226
Madam President, thank you very much.

149
00:14:46,206 --> 00:14:51,489
Councilor, do you think it would make sense, number one, for us to look at this as an ordinance or as a zoning issue?

150
00:14:53,750 --> 00:14:56,552
Are you directing me or to one of the councilors?

151
00:14:56,612 --> 00:14:58,433
No, you, Councilor.

152
00:14:58,733 --> 00:14:59,133
I'm sorry.

153
00:14:59,454 --> 00:15:00,074
Oh, sorry.

154
00:15:04,610 --> 00:15:15,159
You know, I think it's worth looking at zoning and thinking about whether or not if you're interested in splitting this into separate ordinances regulating public and private.

155
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:26,809
Zoning doesn't usually regulate the use of public waste, so zoning's not going to normally, in most instances, regulate public shade trees because they're within public ways.

156
00:15:26,869 --> 00:15:29,892
But if you decide that you want to split this out,

157
00:15:30,410 --> 00:15:37,955
we could give some consideration to whether or not it would best fit into existing zoning or whether or not it would still be better to be a general side ordinance.

158
00:15:39,256 --> 00:15:43,358
But that would probably only make sense if you were only seeking to regulate the private side.

159
00:15:43,398 --> 00:15:55,066
And then to the point that you made earlier, Councilor, we can look at whether or not this is something that might just fit into site plan regulation if you're only trying to reach what trigger site plan as opposed to something different.

160
00:15:55,086 --> 00:15:56,667
So there are all things that we can look at.

161
00:15:57,106 --> 00:16:04,271
as we get some more direction on the real details of what you're trying to regulate from a land use perspective.

162
00:16:05,392 --> 00:16:16,661
And then on that note, we have also included in this proposal a initiative to create a advisory commission.

163
00:16:16,981 --> 00:16:18,162
And I think that's a great idea.

164
00:16:18,222 --> 00:16:19,723
It's something that I wholeheartedly support.

165
00:16:21,104 --> 00:16:24,747
I think that where we're gonna get stuck up in the weeds is really gonna be in the details of

166
00:16:25,735 --> 00:16:35,123
this ordinance relative to what the specifics are regarding the permitting and regulatory functions of the tree removal, not necessarily whether or not we need an advisory commission.

167
00:16:35,503 --> 00:16:40,367
Would it make sense for us to take the advisory commission out of this ordinance and deal with that as a separate piece of paper?

168
00:16:41,108 --> 00:16:47,834
You know, ultimately, especially if you decide to put some of the regulation into the zoning ordinance itself.

169
00:16:48,214 --> 00:16:53,078
So once we have a better handle on really how this is going to go forward, then we can help

170
00:16:53,849 --> 00:17:02,051
advise about the best ways to do it, but it may be that it ends up being the combination of general and zoning regulation, maybe a general ordinance to create the advisory committee.

171
00:17:02,071 --> 00:17:10,853
And it may be some, you know, zoning within the realm of site plan that ultimately makes the most sense, you know, but it may not.

172
00:17:10,913 --> 00:17:17,874
So once we have a better sense of the degree of regulation and how it all fits together, we can help advise on that.

173
00:17:18,914 --> 00:17:19,174
Got it.

174
00:17:19,454 --> 00:17:20,034
Thank you very much.

175
00:17:20,995 --> 00:17:21,715
Thank you, Councilor.

176
00:17:21,935 --> 00:17:22,615
Vice President Behrs.

177
00:17:23,978 --> 00:17:46,766
Thank you, Madam President, and to Councilor Knight and Attorney Stein's points, I think closer to the end of this meeting, I'm planning to make a motion that basically that we look at a, we take the content that's in here and we look at distributing it, or the council would look at distributing it.

178
00:17:46,786 --> 00:17:48,186
I think we're losing you, Vice President Behrs.

179
00:17:48,226 --> 00:17:48,787
Nope, that's me.

180
00:17:49,447 --> 00:17:49,847
Nope, it's me.

181
00:17:52,308 --> 00:17:52,888
Can folks hear me?

182
00:17:53,658 --> 00:17:53,818
Yes.

183
00:17:54,399 --> 00:17:54,579
Yes.

184
00:17:54,779 --> 00:17:55,019
Okay.

185
00:17:55,680 --> 00:18:05,087
Um, sorry, just basically saying that I'd like to distribute kind of everything that's packed into one ordinance here across the relevant sections of the current ordinances.

186
00:18:05,187 --> 00:18:11,992
So, you know, the, I think to Attorney Stein's point, the chapter 87, you know, really look at updating the public shade tree.

187
00:18:12,773 --> 00:18:18,519
Um, if we think that the private, you know, looking at the private properties best done through the zoning ordinance and that could go there.

188
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:24,906
The tree committee could then fall, you know where it would be in under our boards and commission section of our ordinances.

189
00:18:25,407 --> 00:18:27,669
So I am planning to make that motion at a later time.

190
00:18:29,467 --> 00:18:34,430
I'm just putting that out there for discussion, so we don't necessarily need to keep repeating.

191
00:18:34,450 --> 00:18:45,657
And I think that that makes more sense as a path than kind of trying to smash all these different pieces into one new section of our ordinances.

192
00:18:45,677 --> 00:18:48,779
Thank you, Vice President Bares.

193
00:18:48,819 --> 00:18:49,479
Councilor Collins.

194
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:51,340
Thank you, President Morrell.

195
00:18:51,500 --> 00:18:54,562
And yeah, I just, I'm happy to hear that.

196
00:18:54,682 --> 00:18:58,084
I think that that's a motion that I would support, you know, in thinking through this,

197
00:18:59,555 --> 00:19:18,461
you know, the content that we have here, which makes for a fairly long proposed ordinance and also, you know, taking seriously Commissioner McIvern's comments about if enacted as written, what a undertaking enforcing this proposed ordinance to the letter would be for the current capacity of the DPW.

198
00:19:18,481 --> 00:19:26,443
I'm also, you know, curious to have that discussion of if breaking it up into parts potentially, you know, because of the legislative process,

199
00:19:27,243 --> 00:19:32,606
phasing those changes in in chunks, if that could at least help with the capacity issues.

